{% commandanatomy
   command="view"
   subcommand="summary"
   input="--input alignment-report.json"
   inputSub="file" /%}

{% aside type="note" %}
This feature is available in SEGUL v0.25.0 and later versions. To check your SEGUL version, run in your terminal:

```bash
segul --version
# Compatible version example:
# segul 0.25.0
```

Check the [Installation Guide](/docs/installation/update/#segul-cli) to update SEGUL to the latest version.
{% /aside %}

Explore a summary report in your terminal, as tables or plots. It shows the same report SEGUI opens after a summary, so you can check results on a remote server or in a terminal session where a GUI is not available.

The viewer opens three reports:

- `alignment-report.json`, written by [`segul align summary`](/docs/cli-usage/align-summary/).
- `read-report.json`, written by [`segul read summary --mode complete`](/docs/cli-usage/read-summary/).
- `distance-report.json`, written by [`segul align distance`](/docs/cli-usage/align-distance/).

SEGUL tells them apart from their content, so a report with an output prefix, such as `uce_alignment-report.json`, opens the same way:

```bash
segul view summary --input Align-Summary/alignment-report.json
```

```bash
segul view summary -i Read-Summary/read-report.json
```

Other files stop with a message that names the file.

{% terminalshot
   src="cli/view-summary-table.webp"
   alt="SEGUL summary viewer showing a table of 300 loci sorted by number of sites"
   caption="The Loci table of an alignment report, sorted by sites." /%}

## Layout

From top to bottom, the viewer shows:

1. **Toolbar**: the file name, the kind of report, and its main counts, such as `120 taxa · 4,060 loci · DNA`.
2. **Sections**: `Overview`, then one section for each kind of item. An alignment report has `Loci` and `Taxa`; a read report has `Samples`; a distance report has `Loci`, `Samples`, `Groups` with grouping, and the `Matrix` and `Group matrix` sections. Each section shows how many items it holds. On the right, `Table │ Plot` marks the current view, and the plot shows whether it draws bars, a histogram, or a heatmap.
3. **Section content**: the overview, or the items of a section as a table or a plot.
4. **Status bar**: the selected item, the chosen column and its value, and the item's position, such as `uce-12 · Sites: 412 · 12 of 4,060`.

The viewer opens on the first table of items, the part most often wanted.

## Overview

The overview lists the totals of the report on the left, with bar charts on the right. Press `1` to open it, and `Tab` to go on to the tables.

{% terminalshot
   src="cli/view-summary-overview.webp"
   alt="SEGUL summary viewer overview with alignment totals, a matrix completeness chart, and a character count chart"
   caption="Overview of an alignment report." /%}

- **Alignment report**: the data type, taxa, loci, sites, characters, missing data, and GC and AT content, with the range and mean of locus length and taxa per locus. Conserved, variable, and parsimony informative sites show their count and share of all sites, with the loci that are fully conserved or have any variable or informative site. The charts show matrix completeness, as the loci with at least each share of taxa, and the count of each character.
- **Read report**: the samples, reads, bases, mean read length, and Q30 bases across every sample, with a chart of reads by mean quality score across all samples.
- **Distance report**: the data type, model, loci, samples, pairs, undefined pairs, the range, mean, and standard deviation of every pairwise distance, the median of the locus means, and the compared sites per pair, with a histogram of every pairwise distance across loci.

## Tables and plots

Every section of items, such as `Loci`, `Taxa`, or `Samples`, can be shown as a table or a plot. Press `v` (or `F2`) to switch between them. Both follow the chosen column, so you can read the numbers in the table, then switch to the plot to see how they spread.

### Read a table

The table has one row per item and one column per statistic.

1. Press `Tab`, or the section number, such as `2` for `Loci`, to open a section.
2. Press `←` or `→` to choose a column. Its header is highlighted, and light lines separate the columns. Columns that do not fit the terminal scroll into view as you move to them, and the title says how many are hidden.
3. Press `↑` or `↓` to select an item. The status bar shows its name, the chosen column and its value, and its position.
4. Press `s` to sort by the chosen column, highest first. Press it again to sort lowest first, and once more to return to the report order. An arrow next to the column name shows the order.

The selected item stays selected when you sort, so the table may scroll to keep it in view. Press `Home` to go back to the first item. Items without a value, such as duplication from a Default mode run, go last.

### Plot a column

Press `v` in a table to plot the chosen column. The plot draws one bar per item, in the same order as the table, with the minimum, median, and maximum in the title. Sort the table first to rank the items, as in this plot of the loci with the most sites:

{% terminalshot
   src="cli/view-summary-plot.webp"
   alt="SEGUL summary viewer bar plot of sites per locus, sorted from the longest locus"
   caption="Sites per locus as bars, sorted highest first." /%}

Press `←` or `→` to plot another column, and `↑` or `↓` to move the selection. Press `v` again to return to the table.

### Show a histogram

Press `h` in the plot to count the items in ranges of the chosen column instead. Each bar shows how many items fall in its range. The range of the selected item is highlighted, and a note below the chart names it. Press `h` again for a heatmap.

{% terminalshot
   src="cli/view-summary-histogram.webp"
   alt="SEGUL summary viewer histogram of locus lengths in ranges of 50 sites"
   caption="Histogram of sites per locus." /%}

The plot remembers the choice, so other columns also open as a histogram until you press `h` again.

### Show a heatmap

Press `h` once more to draw every column as a heatmap, one row per item in the table order. Each column is colored from its lowest to its highest value, and missing values show as `·`. The line below the heatmap shows the colors from low to high and the selected item's value in the chosen column. Choose the colors with `--colormap`. Press `h` again to return to the bars.

### Columns

Alignment reports have these columns:

- **Loci**: taxa, sites, conserved, variable, and parsimony informative sites, missing data (%), and GC content (%) for DNA.
- **Taxa**: loci the taxon appears in, characters, missing characters, missing data (%), and GC content (%) for DNA.

{% terminalshot
   src="cli/view-summary-reads.webp"
   alt="SEGUL summary viewer table of six FASTQ samples with reads, bases, GC content, and quality"
   caption="The Samples table of a read report." /%}

Read reports list each sample's reads, bases, mean read length, N50, GC content, N content, Q20 and Q30 bases, mean quality, and reads with mean quality below 20. Duplication, adapters, and poly-G appear when the report has them.

Distance reports have these columns:

- **Loci**: samples, sites, pairs, undefined pairs, the mean, median, standard deviation, minimum, and maximum distance, and the mean compared sites per pair.
- **Samples**: loci, pairs, and the mean, standard deviation, minimum, and maximum distance to every other sample. `Nearest` is the distance to the nearest sample. With groups, `Max within` and `Min between` are the highest distance to a sample of the same group and the lowest to another group.
- **Groups**: samples, pairs within the group, the mean, standard deviation, and maximum distance within the group, and the mean distance to the nearest group.

## Matrices

A distance report also has a `Matrix` section with the mean distance between every pair of samples, and a `Group matrix` section with the mean distance within and between groups. Rows are named, and columns are numbered by row. Press the arrow keys to move between cells; the status bar names the two items and their distance. Cells are colored from the lowest to the highest distance, leaving out each item with itself. Missing values show as `·`.

## Key bindings

Press `F1` or `?` in the viewer to see them.

| Keys                     | Action                                                      |
| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| `Tab` / `Shift+Tab`      | Next / previous section                                     |
| `1` to `9`               | Go to a section                                             |
| `v` or `F2`              | Switch between table and plot                               |
| `h`                      | Cycle the plot: bars, histogram, heatmap                    |
| `←` / `→`                | Choose a column                                             |
| `s`                      | Sort by the column: highest first, lowest first, report order |
| `↑` / `↓`                | Move the selection                                          |
| Arrows in a matrix       | Move between cells                                          |
| `PgUp` / `PgDn`          | Move a page (`fn+↑` / `fn+↓` on macOS)                      |
| `Home` / `End`           | First / last item (`fn+←` / `fn+→` on macOS)                |
| Mouse wheel              | Scroll                                                      |
| `F1` or `?`              | Show the key bindings                                       |
| `q`, `Esc`, or `Ctrl+C`  | Quit                                                        |

## All options

| Option          | Description                                                                                  |
| --------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `-i`, `--input` | `alignment-report.json` from `segul align summary`, `read-report.json` from `segul read summary --mode complete`, or `distance-report.json` from `segul align distance` |
| `--theme`       | Colors for a `dark` (default) or `light` terminal                                            |
| `--colormap`    | Heatmap colors: `viridis` (default), `cividis`, `magma`, `inferno`, `plasma`, `blues`, `greens`, `ylgnbu`, `ylorrd`, `rdbu`, `puor`, or `brbg` |
| `--log`         | Log file path. Default: `segul.log`                                                          |
